<resources>
    <!-- Pre-Compose window background, matching the Arctic (light) surface so launch
         shows the brand colour instead of a white flash. Compose drives all in-app colour. -->
    <color name="sterna_window_background">#F7F8FA</color>
    <!-- Splash background. Follows day/night (Codeberg #9: an always-light splash is a
         flashbang on a dark-themed device); the night variant matches the dark window
         background so the splash hands off seamlessly. -->
    <color name="sterna_splash_background">#F4F6F8</color>
    <!-- Splash tern silhouette: dark teal on the light field, soft teal (the dark-mode
         primary) on the dark one. -->
    <color name="sterna_splash_tern">#17414C</color>
    <!-- Home-screen widget. These four values are the widget's palette, taken from the app's own
         schemes in ui/theme/Color.kt and pinned there by WidgetPaletteTest: surface, primary,
         onSurface and onSurfaceVariant. ⛔ Not the splash nuancier above, which is a different
         picture with a different job — reading it here is the defect this file already had once.

         ⭐ THESE VALUES ARE THE TRUTH OF WHAT NOBODY DRAWS, and that is for ever: the initialLayout
         the system inflates when a cell is dropped, and the two previews of the widget picker. No
         provider runs for either, so whatever is written here is what is seen.

         ⛔ AND NOTHING ELSE READS THEM ANY MORE. Every cell the app itself draws is painted in
         Kotlin from the theme the reader chose IN the app — WidgetThemeReader.palette, through
         setBackgroundResource and setTextColor — so it honours the forced light/dark mode, the
         pure-black option (#117) and Material You, none of which a values-night qualifier can see.
         The debt this note used to record is closed.

         ⛔ Which is exactly why these four stay, and why they still follow values-night: the two
         surfaces above are the ones NO provider ever paints, and the system's own day/night mode is
         the only answer available there. A cell dropped on a dark home screen by a reader whose app
         is forced to light therefore shows dark for the moment before the first draw lands; that is
         the initialLayout, and it is the honest cost of having one. -->
    <color name="widget_unread_background">#F7F8FA</color>
    <color name="widget_unread_count">#2F5E59</color>
    <!-- Two roles, not one. widget_unread_count is the ACCENT (Material `primary`): the big
         unread figure and the unread dot of a row. widget_row_primary is a row's primary TEXT
         (Material `onSurface`), like the sender line the app's own list draws. ⛔ Do not merge
         them back: one resource for both painted every row name teal. -->
    <color name="widget_row_primary">#2B313A</color>
    <color name="widget_unread_label">#51606E</color>
</resources>
